SO[Move list from Andries Brouwer's game archive, https://homepages.cwi.nl/~aeb/go/games/games/AlphaGo/LeeSedol/4.sgf, checked 2026-09-13 against Wikipedia, "AlphaGo versus Lee Sedol" (180 moves, W+R, move 78 the wedge at L11, first 11 moves identical to game 2). Comments are Go Master's own.]
C[Game 4 of the 2016 match. AlphaGo (Black) against Lee Sedol 9p (White). Use the arrows or the ← → keys to step through; click any move in the list to jump to it.]
;B[pd]C[Q16: the 4-4 point (fourth line from both edges). AlphaGo, playing Black, takes a corner. Corners first: the edges help you surround points.];W[dp]C[D4: Lee Sedol answers in the opposite corner, also on the 4-4 point.];B[cd]C[C16: the 3-4 point, a step closer to the edge than the 4-4. Both are the standard corner moves; the 3-4 leans towards territory, the 4-4 towards speed.];W[qp]C[R4: the last empty corner. Four corners in four moves is how most games begin.];B[op]C[P4: the first approach. Black comes close to the white corner stone rather than taking a side.];W[oq]C[P3: White attaches underneath the approaching stone. What follows is a standard corner sequence, a joseki.];B[nq];W[pq];B[cn]C[C6: with the corner settled for now, Black approaches the other white corner.];W[fq]
;B[mp]C[N4: the first eleven moves of this game are the same as in game 2 of the match, where Lee also had White.];W[po]C[Q5: here the games part. Read on with the arrow keys; the move list on the right follows you.];B[iq]C[J3: Black extends along the bottom side, and the opening is over: all four corners are claimed and each side has begun to build.];W[ec];B[hd];W[cg];B[ed];W[cj];B[dc];W[bp]
